// The logic-side vocabulary. Nothing here is executable.
//
// Predicates exist so that a contract reads as a sentence rather than as a
// wall of quantifiers. Less obviously, it also means every obligation in the
// package funnels through the same few shapes, so there is one place to fix
// when a definition turns out to be wrong.

///|
/// `i` is a legal index into a container of `n` elements.
pub predicate in_bounds(i : Int, n : Int) {
  (0 <= i) && (i < n)
}

///|
/// Entry `i` of `xs` has been decided.
///
/// `-1` is the sentinel, and the reason `assigned` can be a predicate about
/// the *value* rather than about whether a slot was ever written: a Why3 array
/// is total, so there is no notion of an uninitialised element to talk about.
pub predicate assigned(xs : FixedArray[Int], i : Int) {
  xs[i] != -1
}

///|
/// Every entry of `xs` has been decided.
///
/// The property the caller relies on to index without checking, and the one
/// `fill_forward` is required to establish.
pub predicate total(xs : FixedArray[Int]) {
  ∀ i : Int, in_bounds(i, xs.length()) → assigned(xs, i)
}

///|
/// Every entry in the prefix `[0, k)` has been decided.
///
/// The loop invariant that carries `fill_forward` from "the first entry is
/// decided" to "the whole array is total". It is deliberately weaker than the
/// obvious invariant, in that it says nothing about *which* value each entry
/// holds, because an invariant tracking exact values generates far larger
/// verification conditions and the post-condition does not need them.
pub predicate prefix_total(xs : FixedArray[Int], k : Int) {
  ∀ i : Int, ((0 <= i) && (i < k)) → assigned(xs, i)
}
